# Taiga Task State Gate (33pol)

Every piece of implementation work tied to Taiga **must** keep task/issue status in sync. **Do not start the next task, issue, or story job until the current item’s Taiga state is correct.**

Use **user-taiga** MCP only. Read schemas under `mcps/user-taiga/tools/` before `CallMcpTool`.

## One active implementation item

- At most **one** task **or** issue may be in an implementation status (e.g. *In progress*) at a time.
- If another item is already in progress, **stop** — park or close it in Taiga first, then continue.

## Required sequence (per task or issue)

| Step | When | Taiga action |
|------|------|----------------|
| 1 | Before any code or file edits | `taiga_update_task` / `taiga_update_issue` → implementation status (*In progress* or project equivalent) |
| 2 | During work | `taiga_comment_on_task` / `taiga_comment_on_issue` on meaningful progress or blockers |
| 3 | After verify (build/tests when applicable) | Update to *Done* / *Closed* + final comment (what changed, how verified) |
| 4 | Only then | Start step 1 on the **next** task/issue |

**Hard gate:** If step 1 was skipped, do **not** implement — update Taiga first. If step 3 was skipped, do **not** pick up the next item.

## Switching or stopping work

- User redirects or you must switch items → set current item to a non-implementation status (*New*, *Needs info*, etc.) with a short comment explaining why; never leave stale *In progress*.
- Pure Q&A with no Taiga item, or user explicitly says to skip Taiga → no status updates required.

## Examples

```
❌ Edit code on task #42, then mark In progress later
❌ Mark task #43 In progress while task #42 is still In progress
❌ Finish coding task #42 and start task #43 without closing #42 in Taiga

✅ taiga_update_task(#42, In progress) → code → test → comment → Done → then #43 In progress
```

## Session reporting

**Start:** project slug, active ref (`taskRef` / `issueRef`), current status.  
**End:** which refs were closed or parked and what remains blocked on Taiga state.
